1
00:00:00,431 --> 00:00:02,373
[SPEAKER_01]: Okay, this is Medford Historical Commission.

2
00:00:02,453 --> 00:00:04,475
[SPEAKER_01]: Today is Monday, September 12.

3
00:00:05,857 --> 00:00:08,759
[SPEAKER_01]: Let me just do our order of business here.

4
00:00:09,280 --> 00:00:17,708
[SPEAKER_01]: Pursuant to Chapter 20 of the Acts of 2021, this meeting of the City of Medford Historical Commission will be conducted via remote means.

5
00:00:18,208 --> 00:00:24,554
[SPEAKER_01]: Members of the public who wish to access the meeting may do so by using the Zoom link provided for in the agenda.

6
00:00:25,703 --> 00:00:34,246
[SPEAKER_01]: No in-person attendance of members of the public will be permitted and public participation in any public hearing during this meeting shall be by remote means only.

7
00:00:34,266 --> 00:00:38,147
[SPEAKER_01]: So we have a busy agenda tonight.

8
00:00:38,667 --> 00:00:44,429
[SPEAKER_01]: First up we're going to start with a preferably preserved hearing for 80 Canal Street.

9
00:00:45,270 --> 00:00:49,131
[SPEAKER_01]: So just to recap and how we got to this tonight.

10
00:00:50,211 --> 00:00:54,493
[SPEAKER_01]: Last month we accepted an application for 80 Canal Street for

11
00:00:55,053 --> 00:00:57,274
[SPEAKER_01]: partial demolition of this structure.

12
00:00:58,155 --> 00:01:09,421
[SPEAKER_01]: Because this structure is on the national register, it is automatically deemed significant and therefore tonight we are having a preferably preserved hearing.

13
00:01:09,441 --> 00:01:15,705
[SPEAKER_01]: And I would just like to remind folks the definition that we'll be using tonight when we're talking about

14
00:01:16,125 --> 00:01:19,088
[SPEAKER_01]: historical significance and preferably preserved.

15
00:01:20,429 --> 00:01:42,468
[SPEAKER_01]: So with regards to historical significance, a building is considered significant if it is importantly associated with one or more historic persons or events, or with the broad architectural, cultural, political, economic, or social history of the city or the Commonwealth, or it is historically or architecturally important in terms

16
00:01:42,708 --> 00:01:53,441
[SPEAKER_01]: terms of period, style, method of building construction, or association with an important architect or builder, either by itself, or in the context of a group of buildings.

17
00:01:54,302 --> 00:02:00,009
[SPEAKER_01]: So as I said, 80 canal is automatically significant due to the fact that is on the National Register.

18
00:02:01,651 --> 00:02:05,154
[SPEAKER_01]: So tonight we will determine whether or not the property is preferably preserved.

19
00:02:05,634 --> 00:02:17,205
[SPEAKER_01]: Preferably preserved means that the commission has determined after an open public hearing that the demolition of the building under review would be detrimental to the architectural or historical heritage of the city of Medford.

20
00:02:17,705 --> 00:02:27,432
[SPEAKER_01]: And just to remind everybody that the commission does not and will not consider the building's condition or any safety issues when determining whether a building is to be preferably preserved.

21
00:02:27,852 --> 00:02:31,155
[SPEAKER_01]: Only the building commissioner can properly address those issues.

22
00:02:36,958 --> 00:02:40,060
[SPEAKER_01]: So what I'll do is I will take a motion from my commissioners.

23
00:02:40,580 --> 00:02:42,721
[SPEAKER_01]: We'll go around for commissioner comments.

24
00:02:43,181 --> 00:02:49,605
[SPEAKER_01]: Then I will open up the floor to resident comments or anybody that would like to comment on this case.

25
00:02:50,285 --> 00:02:55,028
[SPEAKER_01]: We'll close those out, circle back for any final comments from the board, and then we will have a vote.

26
00:02:56,249 --> 00:03:01,232
[SPEAKER_01]: So commissioners, I will take a vote for 80 Canal Street.

27
00:03:01,572 --> 00:03:03,613
[SPEAKER_01]: If somebody would like to make a motion, excuse me, a motion.

28
00:03:18,332 --> 00:03:18,932
[SPEAKER_08]: Madam Chair?

29
00:03:19,573 --> 00:03:20,273
[SPEAKER_01]: Yes, Ryan.

30
00:03:20,513 --> 00:03:26,697
[SPEAKER_04]: I make a motion to find 80 Canal Street, preferably preserved with the caveat that I'm going to shoot it down.

31
00:03:27,498 --> 00:03:28,639
[SPEAKER_01]: Okay, thank you, Ryan.

32
00:03:29,279 --> 00:03:30,960
[SPEAKER_01]: Would anyone like to second that motion?

33
00:03:31,821 --> 00:03:33,642
[SPEAKER_05]: Given the caveat, I will second.

34
00:03:34,462 --> 00:03:35,403
[SPEAKER_01]: Okay, thank you, Ed.

35
00:03:36,083 --> 00:03:38,505
[SPEAKER_01]: Ryan, I will start with you then for comments.

36
00:03:39,285 --> 00:03:46,450
[SPEAKER_04]: I think the building has lost integrity to the point where it's no longer

37
00:03:49,864 --> 00:03:56,532
[SPEAKER_04]: In the interest of the city to preserve or to seek any sort of preservation or rehabilitation aspects of the existing building.

38
00:03:57,573 --> 00:04:06,162
[SPEAKER_04]: But I, I am just curious if there are any people from the general public here for the public hearing so I'll leave it at that for now, and then circle back once I hear public comments.

39
00:04:06,923 --> 00:04:10,708
[SPEAKER_01]: Okay, just so you know you cut out a little bit so I would just.

40
00:04:12,030 --> 00:04:14,191
[SPEAKER_01]: Don't move when you're speaking.

41
00:04:15,531 --> 00:04:21,173
[SPEAKER_01]: I'm just going to reiterate, I think the gist of your comment was there's really nothing left here to preserve.

42
00:04:21,793 --> 00:04:22,074
[SPEAKER_08]: Right.

43
00:04:22,414 --> 00:04:22,794
[SPEAKER_04]: Correct.

44
00:04:24,134 --> 00:04:31,657
[SPEAKER_04]: I'm interested in starting the discussion so we can hear what the public has to say.

45
00:04:32,717 --> 00:04:33,057
[SPEAKER_01]: Got it.

46
00:04:33,197 --> 00:04:34,058
[SPEAKER_01]: Thank you very much.

47
00:04:36,278 --> 00:04:38,719
[SPEAKER_01]: Ed, do you have anything to add since you seconded the motion?

48
00:04:41,546 --> 00:04:57,383
[SPEAKER_05]: From what I have seen, although I'm willing to hear otherwise, the district makes sense, location makes sense, but the building, from what I've seen, is not there anymore.

49
00:04:57,403 --> 00:05:00,426
[SPEAKER_01]: Okay, thank you, Ed.

50
00:05:01,768 --> 00:05:02,929
[SPEAKER_01]: Peter, anything to add?

51
00:05:04,779 --> 00:05:19,006
[SPEAKER_03]: No, I think it does have a very interesting history and it is very significant historically for the town with its association with the canal, Middlesex Canal.

52
00:05:21,688 --> 00:05:22,868
[SPEAKER_03]: It's quite an old building.

53
00:05:23,689 --> 00:05:28,231
[SPEAKER_03]: They thought it was a tavern at one point, but it's really an interesting building.

54
00:05:28,651 --> 00:05:33,234
[SPEAKER_03]: But I think I agree with the other commissioners that due to the fire,

55
00:05:34,610 --> 00:05:46,132
[SPEAKER_03]: et cetera, it would be, I don't, you know, I think the city has already lost the asset, I'll say.

56
00:05:46,412 --> 00:05:58,654
[SPEAKER_03]: So that's where I come down, but also I would be very interested to hear from any of the people in the neighborhood or any other citizens.

57
00:06:00,194 --> 00:06:01,255
[SPEAKER_01]: Okay, thanks, Peter.

58
00:06:02,275 --> 00:06:03,875
[SPEAKER_01]: Jess, do you have anything to add on this one?

59
00:06:05,023 --> 00:06:16,968
[SPEAKER_06]: I just have a question, which is, is this on the National Register of Historic Places that some part of the documentation that we reviewed was was the National Register of Historic Places documentation and I wasn't sure.

60
00:06:16,988 --> 00:06:28,132
[SPEAKER_06]: How that relate because it was a whole group it wasn't just one building, it was a whole group of buildings, so if one of the commissioners could just kind of explain that I would really like to learn.

61
00:06:31,074 --> 00:06:32,354
[SPEAKER_04]: And a clarification.

62
00:06:32,874 --> 00:06:34,115
[SPEAKER_01]: Yes, Ryan go ahead, thank you.

63
00:06:34,722 --> 00:06:37,723
[SPEAKER_04]: It's a contributing resource to the overall district.

64
00:06:38,383 --> 00:06:42,144
[SPEAKER_04]: So the NR form I gave you is for the whole district.

65
00:06:43,025 --> 00:06:52,367
[SPEAKER_04]: And the building is a part of that, even though the individual form listed in MACRS does not show it as being a part of the NR.

66
00:06:56,309 --> 00:07:03,631
[SPEAKER_04]: And there are, just so you know, there are hundreds of buildings that are perceived to get,

67
00:07:06,193 --> 00:07:07,174
[SPEAKER_01]: Hold on a second, Ed.

68
00:07:07,514 --> 00:07:08,555
[SPEAKER_01]: Ryan, finish your thought.

69
00:07:09,015 --> 00:07:14,078
[SPEAKER_04]: I was just going to say there are hundreds of buildings listed in Medford, but that's mainly for the canal prism.

70
00:07:14,418 --> 00:07:18,640
[SPEAKER_04]: So those are just considered non-contributing buildings.

71
00:07:18,660 --> 00:07:24,283
[SPEAKER_04]: This is really actually the only one contributing building of the entire NR district.

72
00:07:24,303 --> 00:07:28,946
[SPEAKER_04]: All right, I'm done.

73
00:07:29,226 --> 00:07:29,706
[SPEAKER_04]: Go ahead, Ed.

74
00:07:29,766 --> 00:07:29,947
[SPEAKER_09]: OK.

75
00:07:30,327 --> 00:07:31,988
[SPEAKER_05]: Ed, go ahead.

76
00:07:32,108 --> 00:07:34,229
[SPEAKER_05]: Again, and just for clarification,

77
00:07:34,734 --> 00:07:44,577
[SPEAKER_05]: The NR district is not a city of Medford Historical District.

78
00:07:44,597 --> 00:07:44,717
[SPEAKER_01]: No.

79
00:07:44,757 --> 00:07:45,077
[SPEAKER_01]: Correct.

80
00:07:45,097 --> 00:07:45,977
[SPEAKER_01]: Jess, does that answer your question?

81
00:07:45,997 --> 00:07:46,758
[SPEAKER_01]: Yes, thank you.

82
00:07:47,538 --> 00:07:47,678
[SPEAKER_05]: Okay.

83
00:08:02,917 --> 00:08:04,479
[SPEAKER_01]: Do you have anything to add on this one.

84
00:08:04,739 --> 00:08:15,729
[SPEAKER_02]: Well, just to say that it clearly was a historically significant building and I recognize we can't take the condition of the building into consideration, that's not our, that's not our job.

85
00:08:17,271 --> 00:08:23,597
[SPEAKER_02]: But the its location, and the fact that it was.

86
00:08:24,839 --> 00:08:29,440
[SPEAKER_02]: presumably a tavern, I think makes it a historically interesting building.

87
00:08:29,460 --> 00:08:32,741
[SPEAKER_02]: I'm interested to hear from the public as to their opinions on this.

88
00:08:34,701 --> 00:08:35,501
[SPEAKER_01]: Great, thank you.

89
00:08:38,142 --> 00:08:42,563
[SPEAKER_01]: So I'd like to open it up now to any members of the public that are here.

90
00:08:42,583 --> 00:08:45,343
[SPEAKER_01]: I think the property owner is here as well.

91
00:08:45,403 --> 00:08:52,705
[SPEAKER_01]: So if anybody would like to speak, if you'd like to raise your hand or make yourself known, I will call on you for public comment.

92
00:08:58,923 --> 00:09:04,554
[SPEAKER_01]: Just I don't see anybody raising their hand i'm just double checking, let me just double check the chat.

93
00:09:06,497 --> 00:09:07,600
[SPEAKER_01]: Anybody going once.

94
00:09:09,884 --> 00:09:10,525
[SPEAKER_01]: Going twice.

95
00:09:13,155 --> 00:09:17,158
[SPEAKER_01]: Okay, then we do not have any public comments on this matter.

96
00:09:17,618 --> 00:09:21,081
[SPEAKER_01]: So I will close the public comment portion of this meeting.

97
00:09:22,282 --> 00:09:32,470
[SPEAKER_01]: So I think unless my commissioners have any other questions or comments, I will go around for a roll call vote on preferably preserved status for 80 Canal Street.

98
00:09:35,401 --> 00:09:36,422
[SPEAKER_01]: We're very good, okay.

99
00:09:37,022 --> 00:09:44,767
[SPEAKER_01]: All right, then we have a motion on the table to find 80 Canal Street, excuse me, 80 Canal Street preferably preserved.

100
00:09:45,548 --> 00:09:46,488
[SPEAKER_01]: That has been seconded.

101
00:09:46,648 --> 00:09:50,811
[SPEAKER_01]: I will go around for roll call vote as I see people on my screen.

102
00:09:51,251 --> 00:09:53,393
[SPEAKER_01]: Please be prepared to unmute yourself and vote.

103
00:09:54,173 --> 00:09:54,513
[SPEAKER_01]: Kit.

104
00:10:00,057 --> 00:10:00,577
[SPEAKER_02]: I agree.

105
00:10:01,558 --> 00:10:03,980
[SPEAKER_02]: It is probably too late to save.

106
00:10:04,874 --> 00:10:05,774
[SPEAKER_01]: So you're voting no.

107
00:10:06,095 --> 00:10:06,695
[SPEAKER_02]: I'm voting no.

108
00:10:07,115 --> 00:10:08,075
[SPEAKER_01]: Okay, Ryan.

109
00:10:08,496 --> 00:10:08,776
[SPEAKER_02]: No.

110
00:10:09,656 --> 00:10:10,036
[SPEAKER_01]: Peter.

111
00:10:10,476 --> 00:10:10,756
[SPEAKER_05]: No.

112
00:10:11,817 --> 00:10:11,977
[SPEAKER_01]: Ed.

113
00:10:13,157 --> 00:10:14,318
[SPEAKER_05]: No for not preserved.

114
00:10:15,098 --> 00:10:15,698
[SPEAKER_01]: And Jessica.

115
00:10:16,959 --> 00:10:17,139
[SPEAKER_01]: No.

116
00:10:18,099 --> 00:10:21,181
[SPEAKER_01]: Okay, so the motion fails zero to five.

117
00:10:21,721 --> 00:10:26,203
[SPEAKER_01]: So the property owner will be allowed to proceed with their project on this one.

118
00:10:26,823 --> 00:10:32,525
[SPEAKER_01]: We will get a letter out to you and coordinate with the building department to release your permit.

119
00:10:32,825 --> 00:10:33,886
[SPEAKER_01]: And good luck with your projects.

120
00:10:39,511 --> 00:10:39,811
[SPEAKER_01]: Okay.

121
00:10:40,913 --> 00:10:41,213
[SPEAKER_01]: All right.

122
00:10:41,273 --> 00:10:41,994
[SPEAKER_01]: Thank you, everybody.

123
00:10:42,014 --> 00:10:44,496
[SPEAKER_01]: All right, we'll continue on with our agenda.

124
00:10:45,297 --> 00:10:48,140
[SPEAKER_01]: We are here for back to new business.

125
00:10:48,741 --> 00:10:51,784
[SPEAKER_01]: We're going to start off with 130 Boston Avenue.

126
00:10:53,405 --> 00:10:56,088
[SPEAKER_01]: Tonight we are voting to release the demolition delay.

127
00:10:56,829 --> 00:10:59,171
[SPEAKER_01]: So just to kind of recap how we got here.

128
00:11:01,633 --> 00:11:06,315
[SPEAKER_01]: I forgive me, I forget which month, but we did receive an application for partial demolition at this property.

129
00:11:06,836 --> 00:11:09,297
[SPEAKER_01]: It was found significant and preferably preserved.

130
00:11:09,777 --> 00:11:17,060
[SPEAKER_01]: The demo delay subcommittee has been working with the property owner to come to a resolution to mitigate the demolition on this building.

131
00:11:17,080 --> 00:11:20,102
[SPEAKER_01]: And I think we are there tonight.

132
00:11:20,242 --> 00:11:26,604
[SPEAKER_01]: If Ryan and Peter would like to walk the other commissioners through the process, that would be great.

133
00:11:26,644 --> 00:11:27,625
[SPEAKER_01]: And then we can take a vote.

134
00:11:32,185 --> 00:11:33,366
[SPEAKER_03]: want to start with that Ryan or.

135
00:11:34,968 --> 00:11:38,210
[SPEAKER_04]: Sorry it cut out real quick, what did you ask.

136
00:11:38,230 --> 00:11:44,976
[SPEAKER_01]: Like to start the conversation and walk through the plans to how we got to where we're at for 130 Boston.

137
00:11:45,317 --> 00:11:47,299
[SPEAKER_04]: yeah give me a second i'll see if I can.

138
00:11:48,900 --> 00:11:54,165
[SPEAKER_04]: i'll see if I can pull up the plans here on my screen, so I can share with everybody.

139
00:12:00,018 --> 00:12:02,604
[SPEAKER_08]: you should be able to share as co-host.

140
00:12:13,033 --> 00:12:14,895
[SPEAKER_08]: I'm just going to take a quick zip through here.

141
00:12:14,915 --> 00:12:20,639
[SPEAKER_04]: 130 Boston Ave is a really ornate Queen Anne Victorian house.

142
00:12:21,079 --> 00:12:33,650
[SPEAKER_04]: And we just wanted to make sure that we really documented the building well and made sure that we mitigated the potential demolition that was going to take place in this house.

143
00:12:33,710 --> 00:12:37,553
[SPEAKER_04]: It did get gutted, but now the owner is working to

144
00:12:38,674 --> 00:12:42,037
[SPEAKER_04]: move forward with their permit and kind of restore and rehabilitate.

145
00:12:42,217 --> 00:12:47,302
[SPEAKER_04]: I'm going to use the term rehabilitate because the term restoration has been weaponized against us.

146
00:12:47,342 --> 00:12:51,165
[SPEAKER_04]: So in this case, this is a, this is a tried and true rehabilitation of this building.

147
00:12:52,026 --> 00:12:56,190
[SPEAKER_04]: It was occupied by a hoarder who passed away.

148
00:12:57,010 --> 00:12:58,612
[SPEAKER_04]: And now we have somebody who's

149
00:13:02,103 --> 00:13:04,144
[SPEAKER_04]: interested in putting some life back into this building.

150
00:13:04,805 --> 00:13:06,426
[SPEAKER_04]: But we wanted to make sure we captured that.

151
00:13:06,466 --> 00:13:10,209
[SPEAKER_04]: So these are the drawings that we started with, just so people were aware of what we received with the application.

152
00:13:10,829 --> 00:13:14,272
[SPEAKER_04]: And we really wasn't clear what the actual full scope of demolition was.

153
00:13:14,352 --> 00:13:17,935
[SPEAKER_04]: So we switched and did some existing conditions.

154
00:13:18,035 --> 00:13:27,822
[SPEAKER_04]: Peter and I documented this building on the hottest day of the year and through both looking at what the existing building was and

155
00:13:30,183 --> 00:13:37,725
[SPEAKER_04]: What we could see in the real estate photos, we're able to reconstruct pretty faithfully what was here before the gut rehab had taken place.

156
00:13:38,525 --> 00:13:43,047
[SPEAKER_04]: And so you can see there's some details very typical to Moses man this fireplace.

157
00:13:46,288 --> 00:13:47,088
[SPEAKER_04]: They have some photos of it.

158
00:13:47,688 --> 00:13:50,989
[SPEAKER_04]: Didn't exist when we were doing documentation, but it does exist in photographs that we could see.

159
00:13:51,830 --> 00:13:53,870
[SPEAKER_04]: These wing walls are pretty common to Moses Man.

160
00:13:54,170 --> 00:13:59,492
[SPEAKER_04]: And then very typical of Victorian houses, they just tuck in rooms wherever they need to.

161
00:13:59,572 --> 00:14:02,593
[SPEAKER_04]: So in this case, it has two forward public bedrooms.

162
00:14:03,593 --> 00:14:06,154
[SPEAKER_04]: It has a space for a domestic servant in the rear.

163
00:14:06,674 --> 00:14:10,595
[SPEAKER_04]: Both were able to access this bathroom that was once here.

164
00:14:10,915 --> 00:14:13,836
[SPEAKER_04]: And then there was some space in the third floor that was both a finish room

165
00:14:14,216 --> 00:14:22,208
[SPEAKER_04]: And then what I like to call it, in the back, it was probably a trunk room because Victorians did not have unfinished attics like we do today.

166
00:14:22,248 --> 00:14:23,670
[SPEAKER_04]: They don't put anything in unfinished space.

167
00:14:23,710 --> 00:14:26,654
[SPEAKER_04]: They would typically have stored it in finished rooms in trunks.

168
00:14:31,072 --> 00:14:36,696
[SPEAKER_04]: But most importantly, we wanted to make sure that we captured all the intricate details that are present on this property, because there's a lot of them.

169
00:14:36,716 --> 00:14:43,081
[SPEAKER_04]: And when you really zoom into these drawings, you can really start to see all the shingling and details on the dormers.

170
00:14:43,482 --> 00:14:53,429
[SPEAKER_04]: And some of this stuff was worthy of preservation and recreating in modern materials to make sure that there was some maintaining of the integrity.

171
00:14:54,790 --> 00:14:58,113
[SPEAKER_04]: And on the opposite side, just zooming over here,

172
00:14:59,373 --> 00:15:07,397
[SPEAKER_04]: As part of the documentation, we documented all of the intricate shingles that were on this whole wall, because there's like five or six different patterns, which is pretty cool.

173
00:15:08,138 --> 00:15:11,639
[SPEAKER_04]: The entire lower course is brick, and that's laid out in Flemish bond.

174
00:15:11,679 --> 00:15:17,342
[SPEAKER_04]: So there's actual record of what is here, both photographs and architectural drawings.

175
00:15:17,622 --> 00:15:21,224
[SPEAKER_04]: And that led us to the modern design.

176
00:15:24,740 --> 00:15:27,061
[SPEAKER_04]: Now I put this on eight and a half by 11, so it's a little bit clear.

177
00:15:27,861 --> 00:15:34,462
[SPEAKER_04]: So as typical in most houses, we don't really have a lot to say in the interior, but some of the interior changes for this house do affect the exterior.

178
00:15:34,482 --> 00:15:37,503
[SPEAKER_04]: So we really had to look at and work with how do things lay out.

179
00:15:38,123 --> 00:15:42,844
[SPEAKER_04]: And so there's some minor tweaks to the floor plan that we had to make to adjust to accommodate windows and whatnot.

180
00:15:42,904 --> 00:15:52,827
[SPEAKER_04]: But the end result is that we get a building on the outside that looks much closer to the original, and all of this is going to be replicated in artificial materials.

181
00:15:52,887 --> 00:15:54,567
[SPEAKER_04]: So it can be done in ASAC, it can be done in

182
00:15:56,468 --> 00:16:06,755
[SPEAKER_04]: Certaintied siding, theater impressions that look like that, and Certaintied also makes a vertical siding, so it will look pretty close to the original design.

183
00:16:06,775 --> 00:16:14,200
[SPEAKER_04]: There are some very minor changes like fireplace on the outside, but you get all the characteristics of the existing building here.

184
00:16:14,220 --> 00:16:15,641
[SPEAKER_04]: You still get these flares, you get these

185
00:16:16,161 --> 00:16:19,485
[SPEAKER_04]: horizontal lines, we still have some of the dormers.

186
00:16:20,105 --> 00:16:27,854
[SPEAKER_04]: The only change to these plans that came to me a little bit late in the afternoon is just to block off this window to get a better layout of kitchen cabinetry on the inside.

187
00:16:27,914 --> 00:16:36,042
[SPEAKER_04]: So other than that one change, which I think the commission can certainly accommodate, everything else was good to go according to Jim Calbee.

188
00:16:36,142 --> 00:16:38,385
[SPEAKER_04]: So I think that this building

189
00:16:39,987 --> 00:16:57,833
[SPEAKER_04]: design has turned out well, and I think the final building, if it's constructed in this way, will be a good example of what you can do, even with artificial materials, because oftentimes people want to just drop in vinyl materials, and you can do a lot more than just simply run them up the side of a house.

190
00:16:57,853 --> 00:17:03,175
[SPEAKER_04]: You can make them work with you and work with some of these older buildings to get a lot more life out of it.

191
00:17:03,275 --> 00:17:07,336
[SPEAKER_04]: And that's basically it in a nutshell.

192
00:17:07,356 --> 00:17:08,717
[SPEAKER_04]: Peter, do you have anything to add?

193
00:17:09,454 --> 00:17:10,175
[SPEAKER_03]: No, I don't.

194
00:17:10,575 --> 00:17:14,017
[SPEAKER_03]: I think you did a great job interpreting that.

195
00:17:14,097 --> 00:17:21,483
[SPEAKER_03]: And I just wanted to thank Mr. Calvi for working with us as best he could.

196
00:17:21,603 --> 00:17:26,106
[SPEAKER_03]: And I think it was a great collaborative effort there.

197
00:17:28,564 --> 00:17:29,024
[SPEAKER_01]: I agree.

198
00:17:29,084 --> 00:17:30,506
[SPEAKER_01]: Thank you, Ryan and Peter.

199
00:17:30,626 --> 00:17:35,369
[SPEAKER_01]: And thank you to Jim and the property owner, Jim Calvi.

200
00:17:35,930 --> 00:17:37,631
[SPEAKER_01]: I think we had some good conversations.

201
00:17:37,711 --> 00:17:42,214
[SPEAKER_01]: I think this is a win for everybody here.

202
00:17:42,595 --> 00:17:44,536
[SPEAKER_01]: And I hope he's proud to do the project.

203
00:17:44,556 --> 00:17:49,640
[SPEAKER_01]: And I hope he gets a lovely house out of it that he can provide for somebody who needs housing in the community.

204
00:17:49,720 --> 00:17:54,784
[SPEAKER_01]: So yes, certainly, hopefully, that everybody's happy here.

205
00:17:54,824 --> 00:17:55,685
[SPEAKER_01]: And it's a win.

206
00:17:56,789 --> 00:18:08,220
